@c ************** Chapter 2 ****************
@node Concepts, Commands, Getting Started, Top
@chapter Concepts

Learning these concepts now will make the rest of the manual easier to
understand.

@menu
* Session::                     A Specific connection to a server
* LYNTINDIR::                   A special directory
* Lyntin Char::                 The special Lyntin character
* The Databuffer::              A repository of previous server output
* Variables::                   Variables stand for values
* Hooks::                       Functions called at certain times
@end menu

@node Session, LYNTINDIR, Concepts, Concepts
@section What is a Session?

A session represents a specific connection to a server, along with its
associated actions, aliases, substitutes, gags and other definitions.
Each session has a name which must be unique.  When a session is
disconnected from its server, it goes away, or "dies."

I lied.  There is one session which is not connected to any server and
which is always alive.  Its name is "common," and it contains any
definitions which are defined when there is no other active session.
@emph{Do not} treat this session as a regular one.  In particular,
@emph{Do not} switch into it or try to make it current.  That is not its
purpose.

@*The reason "common" exists is so that you can have definitions which

@enumerate
@item
exist apart from any specific server connection
@item
are inherited by all newly created sessions
@end enumerate

Thus, if you have definitions which you want to share between all active
sessions, you should load them into common, usually at
startup@footnote{alternatively you could do '#all #al name expansion'}.

@node LYNTINDIR, Lyntin Char, Session, Concepts
@section LYNTINDIR, The home of Lyntin

Before you can use Lyntin, you must set the environment variable
LYNTINDIR to the root directory of the Lyntin installation.

The most important consequence of this setting is its impact on Lyntin's
notion of a file@footnote{or directory}. In the course of using Lyntin,
whenever you specify a file which is @emph{not} a full pathname, Lyntin
will assume you mean a file relative to LYNTINDIR.  Full pathnames will
be treated like full pathnames.

@*Other useful properties of LYNTINDIR:

@itemize -
@item
it allows Lyntin to find its own code
@item
it allows Lyntin to find its standard library
@end itemize

@node Lyntin Char, The Databuffer, LYNTINDIR, Concepts
@section The Lyntin Character

The Lyntin character is a special character which notifies Lyntin that
you have typed a command meant for Lyntin, rather than your server.  By
default the Lyntin character is '#'.  So, for example, when you type the
command #alias, Lyntin knows that you are sending it a
command.

You can change the Lyntin character with the command #char.
@xref{Miscellaneous, #char}.

@node The Databuffer, Variables, Lyntin Char, Concepts
@section The Databuffer

The databuffer is a repository of previous output from the server.  It
is useful both from python programs and from user commands.  Think of it
as a list: each time a chunk of output comes from the server, it is
added to the front of the list.

Lyntin provides commands for Searching the Databuffer and setting its
size. @xref{Databuffer}

Whenever any output from the server is added to the Databuffer, the hook
@code{data_hook} is run @xref{Event Hooks}.

@node Variables, Hooks, The Databuffer, Concepts
@section Variables

There are two types of variables within Lyntin:@footnote{Actually, there
are 3 types of variables, if you count Python variables, which of course
You should :-).} pattern variables and Lyntin variables.

@itemize @bullet
@item
A pattern variable "matches" a portion of text, and can be
used later to refer that that portion.  Pattern variables look like
this: %1, %2, etc.  For example if you specify an action @xref{Actions},
whose left side looks like this:
@example
@{%1 tells you '%2'@}
@end example
and then the server sends you
@example
John tells you 'You disgust me'
@end example
then in the right hand side of the action, %1 will expand to 'John', and
%2 will expand to 'You disgust me'.

@item
A Lyntin Variable is a variable with a value within the Lyntin system.
Lyntin variables look like $name and can be used within Lyntin
commands. @xref{Miscellaneous, #var}.

@end itemize

@node Hooks,  , Variables, Concepts
@section Hooks

@itemize @bullet
@item
A hook is a (possibly empty) list of user-defined functions, which
lyntin calls at certain times to allow the user a chance to customize
lyntin's behavior.
@item
Lyntin is sprinkled with dozens of hooks, each of which is called
at a certain time.
@item
a list of all hooks can be found in the file hooks.py, but each is
described in this manual at the most appropriate place.
@item
each hook will receive an argument tuple @xref{Hook List}.
@item
@mtindex hook add
add a function to a hook by calling the hook's 'add' method with the
function as an argument
@item
@mtindex hook clear
clear a hook of all functions by calling the hook's 'clear' method.
@end itemize

@noindent
Example:
@example
hooks.death_hook.add(my_death_function)
@end example

This adds the function my_death_function to the list of functions in the
hook death_hook.  Remember, my_death_function must be a predeclared
function which accepts the right type of argument tuple.


@c ************** Chapter 3 ****************
@node Commands, Python, Concepts, Top
@chapter Commands

Lyntin contains a wealth of user-level commands designed to make
your online experience more convenient.

@sp 1

In this manual, command descriptions look like this:@*
command arg1 [arg2] [arg3]  etc.@*

@sp 1

where command is the name of the command, arg1 is a required
argument, and arg2 and arg3 are optional arguments.

@noindent
All lyntin commands can be sequenced with ';'.

@noindent
Examples:
@example
l n;l e;l s;l w  -- sends one command, then the next, etc.
#al jj jump;#al bb bounce
@end example

@noindent
All Lyntin commands can be abreviated, except #quit and #clear.

Invoking any command causes that command's command hook to be run
@xref{Command Hooks}.

@menu
* Sessions::                    For creating and manipulating sessions
* Aliases::                     For abbreviating commonly typed phrases
* Actions::                     For automatically triggering a response
* Substitutes::                 For doctoring up server output
* Databuffer::                  For operating on the databuffer
* Ticker::                      For monitoring server ticks
* Recording::                   For keeping a record of events
* State::                       For saving and restoring sessions
* History::                     A list of previous commands
* Miscellaneous::               Other stuff which won't fit anywhere else
@end menu

@c ****************** SESSIONS *************************
@node Sessions, Aliases, Commands, Commands
@section Sessions

Don't know what a Session is? @xref{Session}

@cmindex session
@deffn Command #session name host port
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
create a session called @var{name}, connected to the server specified by
@var{host} and @var{port}.  If the connection succeeds, the hook
@code{connect_succeeded_hook} is run.  If it fails, the hook
@code{connect_failed_hook} is run @xref{Event Hooks}.
@end itemize

@cmindex session name
@deffn Special-Command #Session-Name [Command]
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
If only one argument is given, This switches the current session to the
session called "Session-Name", Assuming you have already created a
session by that name.
@item
If @var{Command} is supplied, three things happen in sequence:

@example
1. The current session becomes Session-Name
2. Command is Evaluated -- this can be any Lyntin command.
3. The current session becomes what it was before.
@end example
@end itemize

@cmindex all sessions
@deffn Special-Command #all command
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
This causes @var{command} to be evaluated in every existing session, one
after another.
@end itemize

@sp 3@*
Examples:
@example
#ses a tdome.montana.com 5555
#myses 
#all say hello
@end example

@cmindex killall
@deffn Command #killall
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
clear all sessions of actions/aliases/subs/gags/variables.  Triggers the
hook @code{death_hook} for each session @xref{Event Hooks}.
@end itemize



@c ****************** ALIASES *************************
@node Aliases, Actions, Sessions, Commands
@section Aliases

An alias is a shorthand for a command you type a lot.
@sp 2

@cmindex alias
@deffn Command #alias [@{name@}] [@{expansion@}]@*
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
with no arguments, #alias simply prints all defined aliases.
@item
with one argument, it prints the alias(es) which correspond to that
argument (wildcard expansion of '*' is supported). 
@item
otherwise, it creates an alias called name which expands to expansion.
from here on out, typing name at the beginning of a command will invoke
the alias expansion, which will be interpreted instead of what you
typed.  The expansion can contain lyntin pattern-variables, which look
like %[number].  The variable %0 contains the entire text typed after
the alias.  Other than that, %n is the nth word after the alias. If the
expansion contains no pattern-variables, and you type in arguments to
the alias, lyntin just appends whatever arguments you typed to the
expansion, and sends that.

@item
when defining an alias, braces are only necessary around the
expansion if the expansion contains semicolons.  however, braces are
advised around both the name and the expansion.

@end itemize

Examples:

@example
#al insult @{say you know, you're a %1, a complete and total %1@}
#al kk kill dog (define an alias named kk which expands to kill dog)
#al lookround @{look n;look e;look s;look w@}
#al @{k@} @{kill@} (typing 'k dog' now sends 'kill dog')
#al k* (print all aliases starting with k)
#al al #al (define an alias named al which expands to #al)
#al kll kill (define an alias named kll which expands to "kill arguments")
@end example


@cmindex unalias
@deffn Command #unalias pattern
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
remove all aliases which match @var{pattern} from the current
session. The * Wildcard is supported in @var{pattern}.
@end itemize



@c ****************** ACTIONS *************************
@node Actions, Substitutes, Aliases, Commands
@section Actions

An action is an automatic response to server output.  Actions are handy
for automating things you do a lot, and speeding up reaction time in
critical situations.

@sp 2
@cmindex action
@deffn Command #action [@{trigger@}] [@{response@}]@*
@end deffn


@itemize @bullet

@item
with no arguments, prints all actions

@item
with a single argument @var{trigger} (with or witout braces), prints all
actions which match @var{trigger} (with wildcard expansion of '*'
supported)

@item
With two brace-enclosed arguments, defines an action.  Basically,
whenever lyntin sees anything from the mud which contains the text
@var{trigger}, it evaluates the text @var{response}.  @var{response} is
handled just as if you had typed it yourself, so it can be plain text to
send to the server, or any lyntin command. 

@item
When defining an action, braces ( '@{' and '@}' ) are mandatory around
_both_ the trigger and the response.  Actions can be 'anchored' to the
beginning of a line by prepending the trigger with '^'.  Then, the
action will not be triggered unless it occurs at the beginning of a
line.

@item
actions can contain lyntin pattern-variables, which look like %[integer]
@xref{Variables}. when lyntin sees a pattern-variable in an action
trigger, it tries to match any pattern against it, and saves any match
it finds so you can use it in the response.  see below for examples.

@item
@var{response} can contain the special variable %a, which means "the
whole matched line."

@end itemize

Examples:

@example
#ac @{^You are hungry@} @{get bread bag;eat bread@}
#ac @{EVISCERATES joey@} @{rescue joey@}
#ac @{%0 gives you %5@} @{say thanks for the %5, %0!@}
#ac @{%1 tells you %2@} @{say %1 just told me %2@}
@end example


@cmindex action
@deffn Command #unaction pattern
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
remove any actions which match @var{pattern} from the current session.
The * Wildcard is supported in @var{pattern}.
@end itemize

Examples:
@example
#unac *fountain*
#unac @{^Bill is here@}
@end example

@c ****************** SUBS *************************
@node Substitutes, Databuffer, Actions, Commands
@section Substitutes

A substitute is something you want to see instead of what
the server actually sent.

@sp 2
@cmindex substitute
@deffn Command #substitute [@{what@}] [@{replacement@}]
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
with one argument, displays all substitutes which match @var{what}.

@item
with two arguments, 
defines a substitute such that, whenever lyntin encounters the text
@var{what}, it will replace it with @var{replacement}.  

@end itemize



Examples:

@example
#sub @{a green dragon is here.@} @{VERY DANGEROUS GREEN DRAGON PRESENT@}
#sub ralph @{cousin ralph@}
@end example


@cmindex unsubstitute
@deffn Command #unsubstitute
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
remove all substitutes which match @var{pattern} from the current
session.
The * Wildcard is supported in @var{pattern}.
@end itemize


A gag is a means for ignoring text. It is useful for shutting up
spammers and people who annoy you.

@cmindex gag
@deffn Command #gag [@{what@}]
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
with no arguments, print all current gags
@item
with one argument, define @var{what} as a gag for the current session.
From here on out, any time lyntin sees @var{what}, it won't display the
line which contains it.

@end itemize

@cmindex ungag
@deffn Command #ungag pattern
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
remove all gags which match @var{pattern} from the current session.
The * Wildcard is supported in @var{pattern}.
@end itemize


@c ****************** Databuffer *************************
@node Databuffer, Ticker, Substitutes, Commands
@section Databuffer

The databuffer is a repository of previous output from the server.
@xref{The Databuffer}.

@cmindex databuffer
@deffn Command #databuffer [size]
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
with no arguments, display the size of the session's databuffer
@item
with one numeric argument, set the size of the session's databuffer to @var{size}
@end itemize

@cmindex datagreplines
@deffn Command #datagreplines regex
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
search for @var{regex} in the current session's databuffer.  print any 
lines in which a match was found.
@item
@var{regex} can be any python regular expression, or just the exact text
you want to find.
@end itemize

Examples:

@example
#datagrep gossip
#datagrep leather.*here
@end example

@cmindex datagrep
@deffn Command #datagrep regex
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
search for @var{regex} in the current session's databuffer.  If a
match is found, print the entire data chunk containing the match.
@item
@var{regex} can be any python regular expression, or just the exact text
you want to find.
@end itemize

Examples:

@example
#datagrep gossip
#datagrep leather.*here
@end example

@c ****************** Ticker *************************
@node Ticker, Recording, Databuffer, Commands
@section Ticker

These commands deal with the server's notion of a tick, which is
the passing of a unit of time.  Lyntin monitors this time by means
of the "ticker."  

@cmindex tick
@deffn Command #tick
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
Queries the ticker as to how much time remains until the next tick.
The time remaining is printed.
@end itemize


@cmindex tickset
@deffn Command #tickset argument
@end deffn



@itemize @bullet
@item
With no arguments, it resets the ticker as if a tick had just occurred.
@item
With an @var{argument} of "on" (no quotes) It turns on the ticker.
@item
With an @var{argument} of "off," it turns off the ticker
@item
With an @var{argument} of "toggle," it toggles the ticker to be either on or
off
@item
With a numeric @var{argument}, the length of a tick is set to that number.
@end itemize





@c ****************** Recording *************************
@node Recording, State, Ticker, Commands
@section Recording

@cmindex log
@deffn Command #log file
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
with no arguments, if the current session is logging, 
lyntin stops logging.
@item
with one argument, start a logging file for the current session in the
file @var{file} @xref{LYNTINDIR}.  From here out any output from the
server to that session will be recorded in @var{file}, and anything that
session sends to the server will be too.
@end itemize

Examples:
@example
#log myfile.txt
#a log myfile.txt
@end example

@cmindex report
@deffn Command #report [file textstring]
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
This command takes zero or two arguments.
@item
with no arguments, print all reports.
@item
if args _are_ present, establish a report which prints any line
containing @var{textstring} to @var{file}.
@item
@var{textstring} may contain multiple words and python
regular expressions.
@item
All word borders are collapsed into a single whitespace.
@end itemize

Examples:
@example
#a #report  -- prints all of session a's reports
#report myfile janey gossips -- records all of janey's gossips
@end example

@c ****************** State *************************
@node State, History, Recording, Commands
@section State

These commands have to do with the state, or "current settings" of the
current session.

@cmindex read
@deffn Command #read file
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
load @var{file} @xref{LYNTINDIR} into the current session as a set of
lyntin actions/aliases/substitutes/gags/variables
@end itemize


Examples:
@example
#a #read biff
#read /usr/local/lib/file1
@end example

@cmindex write
@deffn Command #write file
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
save the current session's actions/aliases/subs/gags/vars to @var{file}.
@end itemize

Examples:
@example
#a #write biff
#write /usr/local/lib/file1
@end example

@cmindex clear
@deffn Command #clear
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
clear the current session of all aliases, actions, etc
@end itemize

@c ****************** History *************************
@node History, Miscellaneous, State, Commands

Lyntin keeps a list of previous commands typed by you, the user.
You can ask it to repeat one of these commands instead of having
to retype it yourself.

@cmindex do historic command
@deffn Special-Command ![NUMBER] [old]=[new]
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
redo a command from the history list.  @var{number} defaults to 0,
so ! and !0 are the same.  If @var{old} and @var{new} are present, all
occurrences of @var{old} are replaced by @var{new}.
@end itemize

Examples:
@example
!
!0
!4 jeff=jay
@end example


@cmindex history
@deffn Command #history [number]
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
with no arguments, print the current history list.  These can be
reexecuted with the '!' command.
@item
with one argument, set the size of the history list to @var{number}.
@end itemize







@c ****************** Miscellaneous *************************
@node Miscellaneous,  , History, Commands
@section Miscellaneous

Other commands:

@cmindex textin
@deffn Command #textin file
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
execute @var{file} as a sequence of commands.  handy for posting messages
and doing character creation, to name a couple.
@end itemize

@cmindex speedwalk
@deffn Command #speedwalk
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
toggle speedwalking for the current session.  If speedwalk is on, any
command which consists solely of the letters nwseud combined with digits
(eg nnnwu, 3e5nnnn) will be interpreted as a speedwalking directive, and
lyntin will send the appropriate number of directional commands to your
mud. If speedwalking is off, lyntin will just send such commands
directly to your mud without breaking them up or interpreting them.
@end itemize


@cmindex showme
@deffn Command #showme
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
display @var{text} on your screen.  useful for sending messages to
yourself.
@end itemize


Examples:
@example
#ac @{%1 tells you %2@} @{#showme %1 just told me %2@}
@end example

@cmindex quit
@deffn Command #quit
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
kill lyntin immediately.  This will cut you off from any servers you
are currently connected to.
@end itemize


@cmindex char
@deffn Command #char tochar
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
the Lyntin character is the character you type before each lyntin
command. (by default, it is '#').
@item
with no arguments, print the current lyntin character.
@item
with a single character argument, change the lyntin character to
@var{tochar}
@end itemize

@cmindex variable
@deffn Command #variable [name] [value]
@end deffn

This command deals with Lyntin variables @xref{Variables}.

@itemize @bullet
@item
with no arguments, display all Lyntin variables for the current session
@item
with one argument, display any Lyntin variables matching the argument, with
wildcard expansion supported
@item
with two arguments, define a Lyntin variable @var{name} whose value is
@var{value}.  Lyntin variables are different from pattern variables in
that they stay in memory as long as lyntin does.
@item
the value of name can be referenced at anytime with $@var{name}.
@end itemize

Examples:
@example
#var water fountain
@end example
@example
#al kk @{kill $victim@}

note the braces.  they are necessary because otherwise kk would be
defined as kill (whatever the value of victim happens to be when you
define the alias)
@end example
@example
#al swv @{#all #var @{victim@} @{%%1@}@}

whoa!! note the nested braces.  A pattern variable must contain as many
%'s as its nesting depth.
@end example

@cmindex cr
@deffn Command #cr
@end deffn

@itemize @bullet
@item
send a carriage return to the mud from the current session.  Useful
when an alias needs to send a return.
@end itemize


@node Python, Library, Commands, Top
@chapter Python

Alright, now we get to the point.  All that other stuff was just fluff.
I mean, we're here to @emph{hack}, right?  Are you with me?!  Onward,
brothers and sisters!

@menu
* Init File::                   Where to put initialization code
* Running Your Code::           Calling Python from Lyntin
* Hook List::                   All the hooks
@end menu


@node Init File, Running Your Code, Python, Python
@section Init File

When Lyntin starts up, it evaluates the contents of a Python file named
user.py, which you should use to initialize whatever python data you
will need in the course of your Lyntin session.  It is in the context of
this file that the #@ command is executed @xref{Running Your Code}.

If you look in this file, you will see a Python dictionary called
user_custom.  This dictionary contains variables whose values you can
change to suit your tastes. @emph{Do Not} remove them or change their
datatypes, or bad things will happen @footnote{As they like to say in
the C world, "the result of this behavior is undefined"}.

The user_custom dictionary contains the following keys and default
values:

@table @asis
@item 'too_many_errors' (20)
The number of errors which Lyntin considers "too many."  Once
the number of errors reaches this number, the hook error_occurred_hook
is called @xref{Hook List}.
@item 'history_size' (30)
The maximum size of the history list
@item 'extra_source_dirs' ([])
A list of directory names, as strings, which will be added at startup to
Lyntin's module search path.  Directory names are interpreted in the
usual Lyntin way @xref{LYNTINDIR}.
@end table

@node Running Your Code, Hook List, Init File, Python
@section Running Your Code

Any self-respecting agent will need to execute lots of python code.
Here's how to do that.

@cmindex execute python code
@deffn Special-Command #@@STATEMENT
@end deffn
@itemize @bullet
@item
this is the biggy.  Execute an _arbitrary_ python @var{statement} in
the context of user.py's global variables. 
@end itemize

Trivial Examples:
@example
#@@for c in 'hello': print c
#@@Putline('this is lyntin speaking')
#@@dead = 'yep'
#@@othermodule.doit()
@end example

@node Hook List, , Running Your Code, Python
@section Hook List

This is a listing of all the hooks in Lyntin @xref{Hooks}.  Note that
each of these hooks receives a @emph{tuple} argument.

@menu
* Event Hooks::              Hooks corresponding to Lyntin events
* Command Hooks::            Hooks called when commands are run
@end menu

@node Event Hooks, Command Hooks, , Hook List
@subsection Event Hooks

These hooks are triggered at various times throughout Lyntin's
execution.

@hkindex death_hook
@deffn Hook death_hook
@end deffn
@example
when a session dies.
arg tuple contains a session instance
@end example

@hkindex action_hook    
@deffn Hook action_hook  
@end deffn 
