@node Scheduler, Examples, Exported, Library
@section Standard Module Scheduler

This is a standard module meant for scheduling and invoking events
based on arbitrary criteria.  It contains two classes:

@clindex Scheduler
@deffn Class Scheduler
@end deffn

The Scheduler class holds a list of events, and is responsible for
querying each event every so often to see if the event is ready to
perform.  If so, it calls the event's @code{perform} method.

@clindex TimeEvent
@deffn {Base Class} TimeEvent
@end deffn

TimeEvent is a base class for defining time-based events, i.e. events
that are triggered at certain intervals.

@mtindex TimeEvent __init__
@deffn {TimeEvent Constructor} __init__(@var{interval}=1, @var{times}=-1)
@end deffn

The constructor takes two arguments: @var{interval} is the length of
time between invocations of the event, and @var{times} is the number of
times to invoke the event before descheduling it.  A @var{times} of -1
means it should be invoked forever.  Don't forget to call TimeEvent's
@code{__init__} method from your derived event's @code{__init__} method.


@mtindex TimeEvent internal_perform
@deffn {Method on TimeEvent} internal_perform(@var{scheduler})
@end deffn

This method does nothing in the TimeEvent class.  It is meant to be
overridden in subclasses of TimeEvent.In the code for this method, you
write what you want the event to do when invoked (this can include
scheduling/descheduling other events).

Next, you create an instance of your TimeEvent with a given
@var{interval} and a given @var{times}. After creating the TimeEvent,
you simply add it to the scheduler, which exists as the variable
time_scheduler in exported.py.  For an example of how to do all this,
see the standard module examples.py.
  
TimeEvent is only one possible event type!  This scheme is meant
to be general, and users are encouraged to add their own events,
possibly for inclusion in the standard library.  To do this you must
create a class which contains two methods.

@mtindex YourEvent internal_perform
@deffn {Method on YourEvent} ready_to_perform()
@end deffn

This should return a true value whenever YourEvent is ready to be
performed, based on whatever criteria you decide. 

@mtindex YourEvent perform
@deffn {Method on YourEvent} perform(@var{scheduler})
@end deffn

This should do the actual performing of the event, i.e. whatever you
want.

